## Wall crossing, quivers and spectral networks

The existing finite-quiver wall-crossing theorem has an exact but narrow scope:
finite-dimensional representations of a finite **acyclic** quiver over
`F_q`, upper-half-plane vertex charges, subobject-first Hall product, HN
filtrations and a dimension completion. Its Euler/skew integrated phase
products are equal for two charges; it includes wild quivers and strictly
semistable objects, but no CY3, orientation data, analytic convergence,
physical Hilbert space or BPS identification. The generated spectrum
automorphisms are formal degreewise. The quiver-with-potential CoHA branch is
different: it uses complex representation quotient stacks and the specified
rational equivariant Borel–Moore/vanishing-cycle coefficients. Keep those
coefficient theories separate.

The current carrier metadata adds an acceptance boundary to this scope: the
root `thm-finite-quiver-hall-wall-crossing`,
`thm-finite-quiver-euler-and-skew-integration-is-multiplicative`, and
`lem-quiver-hardernarasimhan-filtrations-exist-are-unique-and-descend` items
are marked published but show only `verification.precheck: pass`. The local
`prop-qftx-hall-spectrum-invariance` item itself has a delegated `verified`
stamp dated 2026-10-22, but it depends on that upstream chain. Preserve its own
stamp without treating it as an accepted proof supplier until the weaker
upstream evidence is reconciled. The finite-field scope and the missing
physical BPS-index dictionary remain separate limitations.

Before a physical wall-crossing formula, define an abelian/dg category or
specified quiver gauge model; its charge lattice `Γ`, antisymmetric Dirac
pairing, central charge `Z:Γ→C`, stability phase, stable/semistable objects,
Harder–Narasimhan property, support/completion conditions and chamber. Define
the protected index `Ω(γ;u)` from a specific Hilbert-space supertrace, its sign
and spin refinement. A wall is the locus where named charges align in phase.
Then state the product order, quantum-torus multiplication, factor
normalization and which index is conserved. A mathematical KS/DT theorem in a
3CY category and a physical 4D `N=2` BPS wall-crossing statement are not the
same theorem. The physical bridge listed in
`def-qftx-cy3-bps-integration-contract` additionally needs a proved comparison
of the physical protected sector with the chosen algebraic invariant. Until
that comparison exists for a named model, do not state the physical count as
an established corollary of finite-field Hall algebra.

For a spectral network, define a punctured Riemann surface `C`, a spectral
cover `Σ→C` (including ramification), the meromorphic differentials/Seiberg–
Witten data, phase `ϑ`, the trajectory ODE for each sheet pair, branch points,
junction rules, finite webs, charge lift to a cycle on `Σ`, central charge,
surface defect and framed/unframed index. State the regularity and asymptotic
conditions that guarantee an actual finite network. The GMN construction is
specialized to class-S theories and its own 2D–4D framed BPS setup. Their 2013
paper describes a geometric argument “if not quite a proof” for the KS wall
formula and explicitly lists unresolved at that date: extension to D/E, the
precise charge-lattice quotient, a K>2 spectrum-generator construction,
refined spin/motivic indices, a precise space of spectral networks, and the
cluster-coordinate conjecture. Those are dated 2013 statements, not a 2026
status determination; review each against later sources before classifying it
as open. A 2026 spectral-network preprint listed below is a candidate source
for an updated finite-web result, but only its abstract was read here.

The `T_3` finite-web calculations in that preprint could supply a useful
dependency-ordered example once independently reviewed. They cannot supply a
general spectral-network theorem, a global BPS spectrum algorithm, or a
physical index identification. Mathematical claims about finite webs should
be proved in a mathematics-domain item; physical interpretation stays a
separately stated conditional/model-specific relation.

## Source and status record

Full-text reading here means the linked PDF was retrieved and selected sections
were inspected; it does **not** mean the entire source or every proof was
audited. The limits below are the actual reading scope for this note.

| Source | What was read and what it supports | Status and limits |
|---|---|---|
| V. Pestun, “Localization of Gauge Theory on a Four-Sphere and Supersymmetric Wilson Loops,” arXiv:0712.2824v3 (PDF title page 20 Sep 2012), `https://arxiv.org/pdf/0712.2824` | Retrieved PDF; inspected abstract/contents and §1 passages containing (1.3)–(1.5), the S4 assumptions around the instanton correction, and the explicit regulator caveat. Section headings and equation locations for §§2–5 were searched to orient later reading, but their determinant/localization proofs were not read in this pass. | Not full paper or appendices. In particular, the author explicitly does not construct the noncommutative S4 deformation and assumes it is well-defined and agrees with Nekrasov's R4 deformation near the poles. Treat the path-integral identity as formal/conditional, not a rigorous continuum theorem. |
| A. Kapustin, B. Willett and I. Yaakov, “Exact Results for Wilson Loops in Superconformal Chern–Simons Theories with Matter,” arXiv:0909.4559v4 (PDF dated 17 Dec 2010), `https://arxiv.org/pdf/0909.4559` | Retrieved PDF; inspected abstract/contents, setup and S3 localization passages in §§2–3, including saddle locus, gauge fixing, zero modes and the Cartan matrix integral. | Not the complete determinant appendices or all examples. The claimed scope is superconformal Chern–Simons–matter, with explicit U(N)/ABJM examples; do not extend automatically to every 3D `N=2` theory. |
| G. Festuccia and N. Seiberg, “Rigid Supersymmetric Theories in Curved Superspace,” arXiv:1105.0689v2 (PDF dated 17 May 2011), `https://arxiv.org/pdf/1105.0689` | Retrieved PDF; read the abstract, §1 and §2 general procedure including the auxiliary supergravity backgrounds and gravitino/Killing-spinor equations; inspected the stated S3×R/S3 qualifications. | Not a complete background classification. Their setup assumes the appropriate off-shell supergravity coupling and notes complex auxiliary/background values and reflection-positivity limitations. Each new background needs its own checked Killing-spinor solution. |
| N. Nekrasov, “Seiberg–Witten Prepotential from Instanton Counting,” hep-th/0206161 (18 Jun 2002), `https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0206161` | Retrieved PDF; read the introduction, §2.2 passages defining equivariant moduli-space integration, §§3.1–3.4 on ADHM, noncompactness/resolution and contour localization, and §4.2's equivariant K-theory/index view. Search hits in §3.5 locate the partition-indexed fixed-point discussion, but the tangent-weight derivation was not read in this pass. | Not a full audit of the gauge-theory path integral or every contour calculation. The paper itself identifies small-instanton and IR noncompactness, uses Ω/noncommutative regularization, and labels the KP/Toda relation conjectural. Instanton count is a formal/equivariant construction under its stated regulator, not a proof of a general continuum path integral. |
| D. Tong, “TASI Lectures on Solitons: Instantons, Monopoles, Vortices and Kinks,” arXiv:hep-th/0509216v5 (19 Dec 2005), `https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0509216` | Retrieved PDF; inspected §2.1–2.3 on SU(N) monopoles, charge sectors, Dirac quantization, the Bogomolny square completion and Prasad–Sommerfield example. | Pedagogical source, not an independent audit of all analytic existence/regularity theorems. The energy calculation is conditional on the specified classical action and asymptotics; the paper's 2005 observational statement is not carried forward as a current empirical-status claim. |
| D. Gaiotto, G. W. Moore and A. Neitzke, “Spectral Networks,” arXiv:1204.4824v2 (6 Oct 2013), `https://arxiv.org/pdf/1204.4824` | Retrieved PDF; read abstract and introduction §§1–3, the open-problems list, and selected §6.5 wall-crossing discussion. | Not the complete derivations or all examples. The authors themselves call the geometric wall-crossing explanation “if not quite a proof”; all cited open directions are qualified as a 2013 source status. |
| M. Kontsevich and Y. Soibelman, “Stability Structures, Motivic Donaldson–Thomas Invariants and Cluster Transformations,” arXiv:0811.2435, `https://arxiv.org/pdf/0811.2435` | Retrieved PDF; inspected the table of contents and introductory §§1.1–1.6. The §2.1–2.3 stability-data/wall-crossing sections were identified by headings/search but their arguments were not read in this pass. | Not the proof sections for the motivic/3CY wall-crossing theorem. The paper's physical BPS comparison is motivation/interpretation; its motivic coefficient, orientation and integrality hypotheses must be read and checked before any exact reuse. The current repository's finite-field Hall proof is a separate local supplier. |
| M. Atiyah and R. Bott, “The Moment Map and Equivariant Cohomology,” *Topology* 23 (1984), 1–28, DOI `10.1016/0040-9383(84)90021-3`; D. Berline and M. Vergne, “Classes caractéristiques équivariantes. Formule de localisation en cohomologie équivariante,” *C. R. Acad. Sci. Paris* 295 (1982), 539–541. | Identified as primary mathematical sources for the compact finite-dimensional theorem; full texts were not retrieved/read in this research pass. | These are proposed source targets for authoring the local Borel/Cartan and fixed-point formulas. Do not claim a source section was read; verify exact theorem locators and hypotheses before item review. |


