## Definition

Use natural units $c=\hbar=1$ and the selected pure four-dimensional rigid $\mathcal N=2$ Yang–Mills model with global gauge group $SU(2)$ and no microscopic matter hypermultiplets. Let $U$ be a simply connected regular patch of its source-proposed Seiberg–Witten Coulomb branch on which the local periods $a,a_D:U\to\mathbb C$ are holomorphic and $\operatorname{Im}(\partial a_D/\partial a)>0$. The earlier Coulomb-branch data define the formal charge-label lattice $\Gamma_{\mathrm{SW}}=\mathbb Z^2$, with labels $\gamma=(n_e,n_m)$ and period pairing $\mathcal Z_{\mathrm{SW},u}(\gamma)=n_ea(u)+n_ma_D(u)$. The source-proposed model convention fixes the scalar central charge to be $z_u(\gamma)=\sqrt2\,\mathcal Z_{\mathrm{SW},u}(\gamma)$; $a,a_D,z_u$ have mass dimension one, $u$ has mass dimension two, and charge labels and pairings are dimensionless.

For the model-specific physical charge data used here, equip $\Gamma_{\mathrm{SW}}$ with the integral antisymmetric Dirac pairing

$$\langle(n_e,n_m),(n'_e,n'_m)\rangle_{\mathrm{DSZ}}=2(n_m n'_e-n_e n'_m).$$

In particular the source-designated monopole label $\gamma_m=(0,1)$ and dyon label $\gamma_d=(1,-1)$ satisfy $\langle\gamma_m,\gamma_d\rangle_{\mathrm{DSZ}}=2$, the convention matched by a two-arrow Kronecker quiver with both arrows directed from the dyon vertex to the monopole vertex. The lattice records formal charge labels only: it does not assert that every label is an allowed line operator or a realized particle.

A physical BPS charge datum additionally supplies, for each $u\in U$ and $\gamma\in\Gamma_{\mathrm{SW}}$, a one-particle rest-fiber Hilbert sector $\mathcal H^1_{u,\gamma}$ of the selected quantum theory or explicitly regulated model, its positive self-adjoint rest-mass operator $M_{u,\gamma}$, and the exact trace domain required by the earlier physical BPS-index interface. Its BPS subspace is $\ker(M_{u,\gamma}-|z_u(\gamma)|I)$. A charge is stable in the supplied chamber data when this eigenspace is a discrete one-particle sector separated by a positive spectral gap from the multiparticle continuum at the same conserved charge; stability is supplied model data, not a conclusion here. With $J_3$ the self-adjoint generator of the massive little-group rotation subgroup, choose the four-dimensional $\mathcal N=2$ second-helicity supertrace convention

$$\Omega_u(\gamma)=-\frac12\operatorname{Tr}_{\mathcal H^{1,\mathrm{BPS}}_{u,\gamma}}\!\left((-1)^{2J_3}(2J_3)^2\right),$$

where $(-1)^{2J_3}$ and $(2J_3)^2$ are defined by the spectral calculus and the displayed operator is required to be trace class on the supplied sector. The stable BPS support at $u$ is the set of nonzero labels with a nonzero stable one-particle BPS sector; it is data of the selected model, not a consequence of this definition or of the formal lattice. The trace-data convention inherits $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ through the planned physical BPS-index interface and its pure-point graded trace-class mathematical supplier; it introduces no use of full Choice. No existence or local constancy of this support is asserted.

For a chosen locally finite set of nonzero charges whose central charges lie, after one fixed phase rotation, in the open upper half-plane, define their phase by $\phi_u(\gamma)=\arg(e^{-i\alpha}z_u(\gamma))/\pi\in(0,1)$. A marginal-stability wall for two nonzero charges $\gamma,\delta$ is the locus in $U$ where $z_u(\gamma)/z_u(\delta)\in\mathbb R_{>0}$; a chamber is a connected component of the regular patch after removing the specified walls. A formal positive cone $\Gamma_+\subseteq\Gamma_{\mathrm{SW}}$ used for ordered products is pointed and additive, and is supplied with an additive degree $\ell:\Gamma_+\to\mathbb N$ that is positive on nonzero charges and has finite sublevel sets. The definition is conditional on the existence of the Hilbert sectors, trace data, cone, and degree; it constructs no continuum theory and makes no Hall-index identification.

