+
+## Definition
+
+Fix an integer $K\ge2$. A **Riemann surface** here is a connected complex one-manifold; assume $\overline C$ compact. A **selected class-S input of type $A_{K-1}$** consists of $\overline C$, a finite marked set $P\subset\overline C$, a collection $D$ of specified puncture defects at $P$, a Coulomb-branch datum $u=(\phi_2,\ldots,\phi_K)$, a selected global form with Lie algebra $\mathfrak{su}(K)$, and a four-dimensional rigid $\mathcal N=2$ model $\mathcal T$ on $M=\mathbb R^4$ with Minkowski metric $\eta=\operatorname{diag}(-1,+1,+1,+1)$, a chosen orientation, and a chosen time orientation. The model is adopted data: its six-dimensional $\mathcal N=(2,0)$ construction compactifies on $\overline C$ with a specified twist, meaning a chosen background coupling of the six-dimensional internal R-symmetry bundle to the tangent-bundle connection on $\overline C$ for which the compactification is taken to preserve four-dimensional $\mathcal N=2$ supersymmetry. This definition does not construct a continuum quantum field theory. Put $C=\overline C\setminus P$. The holomorphic cotangent line bundle of $\overline C$ is $K_{\overline C}$. The sections $\phi_r$ of $K_{\overline C}^{\otimes r}$, for $2\le r\le K$, are meromorphic, have poles only in $P$, and have pole orders and residues fixed by $D$. The selected global form and allowed line and surface operators are part of the model data. Thus the coefficients have the units of the corresponding powers of mass when the Seiberg–Witten one-form is assigned mass dimension one. Use $c=\hbar=1$ and retain the model's trace, charge, and central-charge normalizations. A four-dimensional **Bogomolny–Prasad–Sommerfield (BPS) state** is a state in a model-supplied one-particle sector annihilated by a nonzero subset of the model's supercharges; when the selected supersymmetry algebra supplies a massive rest-fiber mass operator $M$ and central charge $Z$, its BPS-saturation condition is $M=|Z|$. No such state or algebraic identification is implied by the geometric data below.
+
+Let $p:T^*\overline C\to\overline C$ be the cotangent-bundle projection and let $\lambda$ be its tautological Liouville one-form. The **spectral curve** $\Sigma_u$ is the compactification of the degree-$K$ curve in $T^*\overline C$ defined by
+
+$$\lambda^K+\sum_{r=2}^{K}p^*\phi_r\,\lambda^{K-r}=0,$$
+
+Write $\pi=p|_{\Sigma_u}:\Sigma_u\to\overline C$ and also use $\lambda$ for the restriction of the tautological one-form. The curve is required to be reduced and to define a finite degree-$K$ branched cover. A **branch point** is a point of $\overline C$ over which at least one point of $\Sigma_u$ has local degree greater than one; the **branch locus** is the set of all such values. Away from the branch locus and $P$, the local roots are one-forms $\lambda^{(1)},\ldots,\lambda^{(K)}$. Remove the poles of $\lambda$ and write $\widetilde\Sigma=\Sigma_u\setminus\operatorname{Pol}(\lambda)$. The geometric charge group is the first singular homology $\Gamma_{\rm geom}=H_1(\widetilde\Sigma;\mathbb Z)$, namely closed integral one-cycles modulo boundaries. Relative charges are classes of one-chains modulo boundaries of two-chains, with the specified endpoints permitted in the marked set.
+
+For distinct sheets $i,j$, a regular **$ij$-trajectory of Wentzel–Kramers–Brillouin (WKB) phase** $\vartheta_{\rm W}\in\mathbb R/2\pi\mathbb Z$ is a $C^1$ immersed path $z:I\to C\setminus\operatorname{Branch}(\pi)$, for an open interval $I\subseteq\mathbb R$, with $\dot z\ne0$ and
+
+$$\operatorname{Im}\!\left(e^{-i\vartheta_{\rm W}}(\lambda^{(i)}-\lambda^{(j)})(\dot z)\right)=0.$$
+
+Orient it so the real part inside this expression is positive. Reversing the orientation exchanges $ij$ with $ji$. A geometric WKB network is generated from the maximal such trajectories issuing from branch points and from the local junction rule: when compatible $ij$- and $jk$-trajectories meet, an $ik$-trajectory may issue from their joint. At a simple branching of two sheets, the local square-root form gives three outgoing trajectory directions. A physical **spectral network** also includes the model-supplied soliton data on its walls; in the usual class-S construction an $ij$ wall carries a soliton index $\mu$. A geometric trajectory with no supplied nonzero soliton data is not thereby a physical BPS state. The WKB phase $\vartheta_{\rm W}$ is independent data from the phase $\vartheta_{\rm fr}$ of a supersymmetric interface unless the selected model supplies a relation.
+
+A **finite web** is a finite connected metric ribbon graph: each edge has positive finite length and a cyclic order at each vertex; each internal vertex is trivalent and maps to a network joint, and each external univalent vertex maps to a branch point. The metric is part of the declared convention. In the Gaiotto–Moore–Neitzke (GMN) string convention, an $ij$ edge has length $L_{ij}=\int_a^b |(\lambda^{(i)}-\lambda^{(j)})(\dot z(t))|\,dt$ along a regular parameterization $z:[a,b]\to C$, and its physical string mass is $L_{ij}/\pi$. In the geometric cubic-differential convention, a lifted T3 edge has flat length $L_{\rm KT}=\int |\lambda(\dot{\tilde z}(t))|\,dt$ on the canonical cover. For a local K=3 sheet pair with $\lambda^{(i)}-\lambda^{(j)}=(1-\omega)\lambda$, the GMN edge length is $\sqrt3 L_{\rm KT}$; these conventions are not silently identified. The graph's map is isometric in the explicitly selected edge metric, with its sheet-pair labels, to compatible network trajectories. Closed edges are permitted. An open web ending at a surface-defect parameter point is instead a relative web. For a closed web, the chosen sheet-lift rule joins its oriented sheetwise edge lifts into a cycle $\gamma_{\rm geom}\in\Gamma_{\rm geom}$. For an open web on a surface defect $\mathbb S_z$, the lifted chain has boundary $z^{(j)}-z^{(i)}$ for its outgoing $ij$ edge, where $z^{(i)}$ and $z^{(j)}$ are the corresponding preimages of $z$. For an interface path $\wp:z_1\to z_2$, its framed charge $\bar a$ is a relative class with boundary $z_2^{(j)}-z_1^{(i)}$. These lifts depend on the stated local branch and junction rules. The physical charge group $\Gamma_{\mathcal T}$ is a model-specified quotient of a subgroup $\Gamma_0\le\Gamma_{\rm geom}$, supplied with a quotient map $\rho:\Gamma_0\to\Gamma_{\mathcal T}$; it is not identified with all of $H_1(\widetilde\Sigma;\mathbb Z)$ by notation alone.
+
+For geometric periods use $Z_{\rm geom}(\gamma)=\int_\gamma\lambda$. A chosen physical class-S normalization may instead use $Z_{\mathcal T}(\rho(\gamma))=\pi^{-1}\int_\gamma\lambda$ for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0$, as in the cited Gaiotto–Moore–Neitzke convention, or another explicitly supplied normalization. A cubic-differential T3 geometry may use $Z_{\rm geom}$; its geometric normalization is not a physical mass normalization. In either case, the central charge is a model datum on the physical charge group, and its dimension is mass.
+
+For a generic point $z\in C$ outside the branch and puncture loci, the selected model may supply a canonical half-BPS surface defect $\mathbb S_z$ in the fundamental representation of the type-$A_{K-1}$ Lie algebra, whose $K$ isolated gapped vacua are labeled by $\pi^{-1}(z)$. It is a codimension-two insertion in the four-dimensional model, in the sense of a model-specified local insertion supported on an embedded submanifold; no topological invariance is assumed. A generic interface datum consists of distinct regular points $z_1,z_2$, a path $\wp:z_1\to z_2$, and a phase $\vartheta_{\rm fr}$, giving a half-BPS interface $L_{\wp,\vartheta_{\rm fr}}$ between the two surface defects. In the convention used here, genericity excludes any nonzero bulk or relative central charge on the forbidden ray $e^{-i\vartheta_{\rm fr}}Z\in\mathbb R_{<0}$.
+
+When the indicated one-particle Hilbert sectors and trace domains exist and the traces are trace class, the **bulk second-helicity index** is
+
+$$\Omega_{\mathcal T}(\gamma)=-\frac12\operatorname{Tr}_{\mathcal H^{1,\mathrm{BPS}}_{\gamma}}\!\left((-1)^{2J_3}(2J_3)^2\right),$$
+
+where $J_3$ is the self-adjoint generator of a massive four-dimensional spatial rotation in the rest frame. The unrefined **surface-defect soliton index** is $\mu(\bar a)=\operatorname{Tr}_{\mathcal H^{1,\mathrm{BPS}}_{\mathbb S_z,\bar a}}F e^{i\pi F}$, with $F$ the specified fermion-number operator. The framed index for $L_{\wp,\vartheta_{\rm fr}}$ is
+
+$$\overline\Omega(L_{\wp,\vartheta_{\rm fr}},\bar a;y)=\operatorname{Tr}_{\mathcal H^{1,\mathrm{BPS}}_{L_{\wp,\vartheta_{\rm fr}},\bar a}} e^{i\pi F}(-y)^{\mathcal J},\qquad \mathcal J=2J_{12}+2I_{12},$$
+
+where $J_{12}$ is the generator of spatial rotations in the selected $1$–$2$ plane, $I_{12}$ is the selected $SU(2)_R$ generator, and $y$ is dimensionless; $y=1$ gives the unrefined framed index in this convention. The choice of fermion-number operator and sign refinement is part of the model data; without it these indices are defined only up to sign. When the chosen sign refinement requires an extended lift of $\bar a$, the index is indexed by that lift and it must not be silently identified with a function of $\bar a$ alone. The geometric network does not determine any of these traces without the specified physical Hilbert sectors.
+
