50:A **finite web** is a finite connected metric ribbon graph: each edge has positive finite length and a cyclic order at each vertex; each internal vertex is trivalent and maps to a network joint, and each external univalent vertex maps to a branch point. The metric is part of the declared convention. In the Gaiotto–Moore–Neitzke (GMN) string convention, an $ij$ edge has length $L_{ij}=\int_a^b |(\lambda^{(i)}-\lambda^{(j)})(\dot z(t))|\,dt$ along a regular parameterization $z:[a,b]\to C$, and its physical string mass is $L_{ij}/\pi$. In the geometric cubic-differential convention, a lifted T3 edge has flat length $L_{\rm KT}=\int |\lambda(\dot{\tilde z}(t))|\,dt$ on the canonical cover. For a local K=3 sheet pair with $\lambda^{(i)}-\lambda^{(j)}=(1-\omega)\lambda$, the GMN edge length is $\sqrt3 L_{\rm KT}$; these conventions are not silently identified. The graph's map is isometric in the explicitly selected edge metric, with its sheet-pair labels, to compatible network trajectories. Closed edges are permitted. An open web ending at a surface-defect parameter point is instead a relative web. For a closed web, the chosen sheet-lift rule joins its oriented sheetwise edge lifts into a cycle $\gamma_{\rm geom}\in\Gamma_{\rm geom}$. For an open web on a surface defect $\mathbb S_z$, put $A_z=\pi^{-1}(z)\subset\widetilde\Sigma$; its lifted chain is a class in $H_1(\widetilde\Sigma,A_z;\mathbb Z)$ with boundary $z^{(j)}-z^{(i)}$ for its outgoing $ij$ edge, where $z^{(i)}$ and $z^{(j)}$ are the corresponding preimages of $z$. For an interface path $\wp:z_1\to z_2$, put $A_{12}=\pi^{-1}(\{z_1,z_2\})\subset\widetilde\Sigma$; its framed charge $\bar a$ is a class in $H_1(\widetilde\Sigma,A_{12};\mathbb Z)$ with boundary $z_2^{(j)}-z_1^{(i)}$. These lifts depend on the stated local branch and junction rules. The physical charge group $\Gamma_{\mathcal T}$ is a model-specified quotient of a subgroup $\Gamma_0\le\Gamma_{\rm geom}$, supplied with a surjective quotient map $\rho:\Gamma_0\to\Gamma_{\mathcal T}$; it is not identified with all of $H_1(\widetilde\Sigma;\mathbb Z)$ by notation alone.
52:For geometric periods use $Z_{\rm geom}(\gamma)=\int_\gamma\lambda$. The physical central charge is a model-supplied function $Z_{\mathcal T}:\Gamma_{\mathcal T}\to\mathbb C$ of mass dimension one. The selected GMN normalization $Z_{\mathcal T}(\rho(\gamma))=\pi^{-1}\int_\gamma\lambda$ for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0$ is available only when $\int_\kappa\lambda=0$ for every $\kappa\in\ker\rho$, so the period formula is independent of the representative of $\rho(\gamma)$. If this descent condition is not supplied, the physical central charge remains a separate model datum and no equality with geometric periods is asserted. A cubic-differential T3 geometry may use $Z_{\rm geom}$; its geometric normalization is not a physical mass normalization.
