100:1.1 In the real basis $(1,\omega)$, the segment displacement is $\widetilde B-\widetilde A=(2/3,1/3)$, or $(2+\omega)/3=1/2+i\sqrt3/6$. Its flat length is $\sqrt{(1/2)^2+(\sqrt3/6)^2}=1/\sqrt3>0$ and its slope is $\tan\vartheta_{\rm KT}=(\sqrt3/6)/(1/2)=1/\sqrt3$. Since $\arg(1-\omega)=-\pi/6$, the ordered sheet difference $(1-\omega)\lambda$ along this path has phase $\vartheta_{\rm W}=\vartheta_{\rm KT}-\pi/6=0$. The source formula therefore uses coprime $(p_0,q_0)=(2,1)$, with determinant $4-2+1=3$, and $\int_p\lambda=(2+\omega)/3$ in the chosen flat coordinate. [given, F1, L1, algebra]
102:2.1 A point on the open segment has basis coordinates $(2s/3,s/3)$ for $0<s<1$. It cannot equal a lattice translate of $\widetilde A$, since that would require an integer second coordinate $s/3\in(0,1/3)$; it cannot equal a translate of $\widetilde B$, since the second coordinate would require $s=1+3n$ for an integer $n$, which has no solution in $(0,1)$; and it cannot equal a translate of $\widetilde C$, since it would require $s=2+3n$, also with no solution in $(0,1)$. Thus the open segment contains no branch point. It is embedded on the torus since a nonzero difference between two points on it has norm less than $1/\sqrt3$, whereas every nonzero lattice vector has norm at least $1$. Its projection under $\pi$ also has no interior self-identification: for $0<s,t<1$, the basis coordinates of $p(t)-\tau(p(s))$ are $((2t+s)/3,(t-s)/3)$, which cannot both be integers, and those of $p(t)-\tau^2(p(s))$ are $((2t+s)/3,(t+2s)/3)\in(0,1)^2$, also not a lattice vector. Therefore the projected path is an embedded regular trajectory from one conical branch value to the other. The WKB network contains this trajectory from each endpoint, in both orientations; the two orientations form a double wall. [given, F1, F2, L1, step 1.1, algebra]
104:3.1 The graph $T=[0,1]$ with its single edge assigned length $1/\sqrt3$ in the KT cover metric is finite and connected, has two univalent external vertices and no internal vertices, and maps isometrically to the projected double wall because $\pi$ is an unramified local isometry on its open edge. In the GMN sheet-difference metric its edge length is $|1-\omega|/\sqrt3=1$, with corresponding string mass $1/\pi$. Thus it satisfies the finite-web definition in either explicitly declared convention. [given, F3, step 1.1, step 2.1, construct]
106:4.1 The deck rotation $\tau$ fixes the two endpoints, so the chain $p-\tau(p)$ is closed. Since $\tau^*\lambda=\omega\lambda$ and $\int_p\lambda=(2+\omega)/3$, its canonical-lift period is $\int_{p-\tau(p)}\lambda=(1-\omega)(2+\omega)/3=1$, using $1+\omega+\omega^2=0$. This nonzero period implies the cycle is nonzero in $H_1(\Sigma;\mathbb Z)$: a boundary integrates to zero because the holomorphic one-form $\lambda$ is closed. The geometric phase is $0$ modulo $\pi/3$, agreeing with $\vartheta_{\rm KT}+\pi/2=2\pi/3$ modulo $\pi/3$ from the finite-web period convention. This proves only a nonzero geometric class and period; it does not prove its physical image or any numerical index. [given, F4, F5, L1, step 1.1, step 3.1, algebra] ∎

