+# Batch 55 — Step 1 authoring evidence
+
+Run: `advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23`  
+Batch: 55  
+Scope: `qftx-spectral-networks` and `qftx-spectral-networks-examples` only.
+
+## Scope and plan reconciliation
+
+The assigned design at `research/plan-advanced-qft-and-supersymmetry-track.md:765` and the corresponding `research/plan-spec.json` rows agree on A/B IDs, title, category, library, order 1011, reciprocal companion, and A-page requirements `qftx-physical-hall-bps-wall-crossing` and `qftx-defects-and-topological-operators`. There is no substantive design/plan conflict. The shared plan and initial manifest have empty item arrays while the exact inventory names `def-qftx-spectral-network-data` and `ex-qftx-t3-finite-web-index`; these are planning placeholders under the owner direction, not permission to leave the pages empty.
+
+The A-page scope is preserved: define class-S spectral curves and their regularity data; distinguish geometric WKB trajectories and finite webs from model-supplied physical BPS data; define the geometric charge lift, physical charge quotient, defect framing, and index conventions; and do not update any dated open status without current source review. The B-page obligation is the source-qualified T3 finite-web example, with its actual charge and defect/index conventions and no general algorithm claim.
+
+## Dependency inventory and current supplier check
+
+The current dependency-level file places batch 55 at page dependency level 8, requiring batch 36 (level 3) and batch 54 (level 7). Item levels are recomputed from actual item `deps`: `def-qftx-spectral-network-data` uses `def-aqft-topological-defect` at level 0, so its item level is 1; the B example will use the A definition, so its expected level is 2. The pure-SU(2) physical BPS data on the required Hall page is not a direct logical supplier for the class-S type-A2 geometric claims and is not added to item `deps`.
+
+Current `loadStep1Record`/`step1Decision` checks found both `def-aqft-topological-defect` and `def-aqft-topological-interface` current and `ready`. The directly reused `def-aqft-topological-defect` statement specifies a model-bound insertion and its correlator data; this batch uses that only to formulate a surface-defect insertion and does not transfer its topologicality criterion.
+
+Fresh current `loadStep1Record`/`step1Decision` checks show `def-qftx-physical-bps-charge-data` and `pthm-qftx-physical-bps-wall-crossing` are both `ready`. I reread their complete current carriers. The charge definition is specifically for a pure four-dimensional rigid N=2 SU(2) model, with a second-helicity trace on supplied BPS sectors. The theorem's complete proof derives its equality only under the explicit finite-field hypotheses and a separate coefficient-, charge-, pairing-, order-, and per-phase Hall-to-physical bridge. This batch uses neither as an item-level premise: its general class-S definition supplies model-specific charge groups and index traces and asserts no wall-crossing identity. The required Hall-page edge is therefore verified as the planned context/prerequisite, without adding an unsupported item-level edge or claiming that theorem's conditional bridge. The current `def-qftx-physical-bps-index` carrier and Step 1 record are also `ready`; its inspected definition is a pure-point Witten heat supertrace, not the second-helicity or framed-index definition needed here. No item edge to it is asserted.
+
+## Full-text source work
+
+1. Davide Gaiotto, Gregory W. Moore, and Andrew Neitzke, *Spectral Networks*, `https://arxiv.org/pdf/1204.4824` (arXiv:1204.4824v2). Retrieved and inspected the full 87-page PDF; read the relevant passages in §2 (class-S curve and branch data), §§3.1–3.3 (trajectory ODE, cycle lifts, surface-defect solitons and framed states), §§4.1–4.4 and 5.1–5.2 (framed-index conventions and wall/junction construction), and §§9.2–9.3 (geometric networks versus soliton content). The source uses the physical period normalization `1/pi`, distinguishes relative from absolute charge classes, flags the sign-lift subtlety, and says general finite-web zero-mode quantization is incomplete. Its geometric wall-crossing discussion is explicitly qualified by the authors as “if not quite a proof.”
+
+2. Clarence Kineider, Georgios Kydonakis, Eugen Rogozinnikov, Valdo Tatitscheff, and Alexander Thomas, *Spectral Networks: Bridging Higher-Rank Teichmüller Theory and BPS States*, final arXiv:2412.03588v3 monograph associated with Lecture Notes in Mathematics 2386 (2026), Chapter 22, printed pp. 365–396, active full-text URL `https://arxiv.org/pdf/2412.03588v3`. The publisher landing page `https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-032-09219-9_23` was not read: its first fetch returned a 303-character interstitial and each of five recovery retries returned a cookie wall. After searching alternatives, I opened the final 514-page arXiv monograph and read the complete corresponding passages in §§22.1.1–22.1.3, §22.2.3 Eq. (22.7), and §§22.3.1.1–22.3.1.4. This independent book-length treatment gives the type-A compactification and spectral-curve setup, local BPS/trajectory equation, `1/pi` mass and central-charge normalization, finite-web and relative-charge descriptions, the second-helicity trace, and framed/soliton indices. It also records the fermion-number sign ambiguity; none of these source statements constructs the selected continuum model or supplies a numerical T3 index. The two publisher endpoint failures, six actual attempts per page route, alternate searches, and per-item replacement arguments are retained in coverage; the publisher URL is dropped while the active monograph text remains sourced and fetch-stamped.
+
+3. Omar Kidwai and Guillaume Tahar, *Spectral Networks, Finite Webs, and the T3 Cubic Differential*, arXiv:2610.23752v1, full-text HTML `https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23752v1`. Inspected selected complete passages in §§1, 2.1–2.3, 3.1, 3.3, and Appendix A, including Definitions 2.2 and 2.4, the complete proofs of Lemmas 2.5 and 2.7 and Lemma 3.1, the rational-slope convention, Theorem 3.12 and its proof, and the phase conversion in Appendix A. The article supplies a proved geometric finite-web construction and nonzero geometric period for its cubic-differential setting. It does not compute a numerical physical BPS or framed index; its T3 results remain geometric and source-qualified.
+
+The current specialist note `research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-localization-bps.md` recorded only abstract-level reading of arXiv:2610.23752v1; this batch independently retrieved and read the relevant full text. The prior note is left unchanged. Coverage marks the Springer delivery URL as a dropped endpoint with its six actual failed attempts per page, four alternate searches, and item-specific replacement arguments under `source_resolution`; the final arXiv monograph remains the active source. Exact source locators, supported items, read extents, limitations, and per-result dispositions are recorded in batch coverage. `source-fetch-check --stamp` passed for the five active page-source records: 7/7 source records are resolved, with two documented dropped Springer endpoints; the three unique full texts are GMN PDF (87 pages, SHA256 prefix `bf01cbaa5a8712c8`), the T3 preprint HTML (66,200 extracted text characters, prefix `a82e5ad90a7455eb`), and the final book monograph PDF (514 pages, prefix `7ebd81e3410dfa98`).
+
+## Item checkpoint
+
+- `def-qftx-spectral-network-data` is authored on the A page as a conditional physics definition. It specifies a type-A class-S input; its spectral curve, sheet roots, and units; the WKB ODE and phase convention; geometric finite webs and charge lifts; explicit relative homology pairs for open defect/interface charges; a separately supplied physical charge quotient; and distinct GMN string and KT cubic-flat metrics. The GMN period formula is permitted only when its geometric period vanishes on the quotient kernel; otherwise physical central charge remains separate model data. It also defines generic half-BPS surface defects and bulk, soliton, and framed-index conventions with trace-class and sign-lift qualifications. Its sole direct item dependency is `def-aqft-topological-defect` (`formulation-prerequisite`, ready, level 0); its item level is 1. It does not depend on pure-SU(2) Hall data or claim that a geometric web is a physical state. The A page's Hall and defects page requirements are retained; both page-level edges have now been checked and recorded, while the item contract uses only the inspected defect-insertion definition.
+
+- `ex-qftx-t3-finite-web-index` is authored on the B companion with the specific T3 cubic differential `dx^3/(x^2(x-1)^2)`, the source-normalized torus cover and ramification points, KT geometric phase `pi/6`, the corresponding sheet-difference phase `0`, and a one-edge web from `A` to `B`. Its complete verification checks the slope/determinant, excludes interior branch points and projection self-identifications, verifies the finite-web graph conditions, and computes the canonical geometric period as `1`. A separate model charge map may annihilate this cycle only when its physical central-charge data permit it; under a geometric-period normalization descending through the quotient, its nonzero period forces a nonzero image. The GMN string formula gives only the normalized value `1/pi` here, not an assigned physical mass. No numerical bulk, soliton, or framed index is claimed. Its sole direct item dependency is `def-qftx-spectral-network-data` (`formulation-prerequisite`, level 1), so its item level is 2. The TikZ diagram depicts the canonical-cover fundamental parallelogram and the lifted edge; the proof does not rely on the diagram. Both source-backed arguments and the current proof contract are present.
